      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The best time to replace a roof in Utah is during fall (September through November) or late spring. During these windows, temperatures typically range between 45\u00b0F and 85\u00b0F, which is ideal for asphalt shingles to thermally seal properly without becoming brittle from extreme cold or overly soft and scuff-prone from summer heat .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "fall (September through November) or late spring"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Seasonal Breakdown for Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Fall (September \u2013 November): Widely considered the peak season. The weather is dry and mild, giving adhesives enough warmth to seal shingles before Utah\u2019s freezing winter temperatures arrive.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Spring (April \u2013 June): Offers comfortable working temperatures and helps you beat the heavy summer and fall contractor rush. However, you must watch out for spring rain showers or late-season mountain snowstorms.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Summer (July \u2013 August): Usable, but high heat can make asphalt shingles soft and vulnerable to scuffing or foot-traffic damage during installation. It is also the busiest time for contractors, meaning longer wait times.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Winter (December \u2013 February): Generally avoided due to snow, ice, and freezing temperatures that make shingles brittle and prevent proper thermal sealing. However, some contractors offer off-season discounts or faster scheduling if an emergency replacement is required.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Utah-Specific Considerations"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "High-Elevation UV and Weather: Utah's intense high-elevation UV rays, Wasatch Front windstorms, and heavy winter snow loads can shorten a roof's lifespan compared to milder climates.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Material Lifespan: Standard 3-tab asphalt shingles last about 15\u201320 years, architectural shingles last 25\u201330 years, and metal roofing systems last 40\u201370 years.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What type of roofing material you currently have or want (asphalt shingle, metal, etc.)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The approximate size or square footage of your home"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can give you a better idea of expected costs and timelines."
      }
